is_int: returns True only for real integers, 
excluding booleans (since True is an int in Python).
read_file: opens the file and returns its full content as one string. 
Raises ParsingError if the file is missing, unreadable, or empty/whitespace only.
strip_comments: replaces lines starting with # 
(after optional whitespace) with empty lines, so JSON error line numbers 
stay correct. Raises ParsingError if nothing remains.
parse_json: loads the cleaned text as JSON. 
Converts a decode error into a ParsingError with line and column, 
and raises if the top level isn't an object.
check_keys: warns about unknown keys in the file.
Returns nothing; missing keys are handled later by defaults.
check_values: validates one global key/value against the rule tables 
(type, minimum, non-empty string for the filename).
Returns True or False and never raises.
check_level: takes one raw level and its index. 
If it isn't a dict, returns the default level.
Otherwise validates each key and replaces bad or missing ones with the default level's values, warning each time. Returns a complete level.
default_level: builds the default level for an index, with size, 
pacgum count, and ghost count growing with difficulty.
normalize_levels: if the input has fewer than 10 levels, 
pads with default_level and warns. If it has more, keeps them all.
build_default: returns a full valid config from DEFAULT_CONFIG plus 10 
generated levels. Used by main after a fatal ParsingError.
parse: the public entry point. It calls read_file, strip_comments, 
parse_json, check_keys, then validates each global key (falling back to its default), then runs check_level on each level, then normalize_levels. Returns the final config dict.